.TH DRAFTPAPER 1 "2026-10-15" "draftpaper 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
draftpaper, draftpaper-compositor, draftpaper-session, draftpaper-greeter, draftpaper-as \- the Wayland desktop drawn as a drafting sheet
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B draftpaper
.I COMMAND
.br
.B draftpaper-session
.br
.B draftpaper-greeter
.RI [ USER ]
.br
.B draftpaper-compositor
.RB [ --tty \fR|\fB --headless
.IR WIDTH x HEIGHT
.RB [ --scale
.IR S ]]
.RB [ --session \fR|\fB --greeter ]
.RB [ --command
.IR PROGRAM ]
.br
.B draftpaper-compositor msg
.I REQUEST
.br
.B draftpaper-as
.I USER
.RI [ COMMAND ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Draftpaper is a tiling Wayland compositor whose desktop is a drafting sheet: paper with a frame, zone
ticks and a dot grid (or a wallpaper in the frame), a live title block carrying the readings, lettered
margins with the workspaces and notifications, and side-margin cells for parked windows.
.PP
.B draftpaper-session
is the session the display manager starts ("Draftpaper"), or that runs from a text console: it sets the
session's environment and runs
.BR "draftpaper-compositor --tty --session" ,
which starts the systemd user target
.B draftpaper-session.target
(the shell, swayidle, the volume keeper) once it is up. Without
.BR --tty ,
.B draftpaper-compositor
runs nested in a window of the running desktop, and with no desktop to nest in it refuses to start: it
takes the displays and input devices only when asked to. With
.BR --headless ,
for tests, it draws one output of that size offscreen on the GPU's render node, shown nowhere
.RB ( "make pixels" );
.B DRAFTPAPER_MM
sets its pixels per millimetre.
.PP
.B draftpaper-greeter
is the login screen, for greetd: it runs
.B draftpaper-compositor --tty --greeter
on the copy of the sheet of USER (or of whoever ran
.B draftpaper apply
last) in /var/lib/draftpaper/greeter, with a sign-in card on it, and starts
.B draftpaper-session
once greetd accepts the password. Leaving, the compositor keeps the bare sheet on screen, and the next
one takes the displays over as they are, so the screen does not go dark in between (see README.md).
.PP
.B draftpaper
is the rest: themes, the generated files for the shell, the launcher, the lock screen and the
applications, and the readings.
.PP
.B draftpaper-as
runs a program as another user on the session (that user's login shell if no command is given), for
work kept under a separate account; it needs sudo to that user and
.BR setfacl (1).
The user is let onto the display through an access entry on the Wayland socket, which lasts until the
session ends: as with
.BR xhost (1)
on X, that is no security boundary.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Apply the theme and run the shell (draftpaper-shell.service does this in the session).
.TP
.B stop
Stop the shell.
.TP
.B apply
Write the theme's files again: the shell, the launcher, the lock screen, idle, the applications.
.TP
.BR theme " [" list "|" next "|" json "|" \fINAME\fR " [" --preview ]]
List the themes, switch to the next one, print every theme's values, or switch to NAME (the sheet and
the applications; with
.B --preview
the sheet only).
.TP
.BR "theme check" " [" \fINAME\fR ...]
Hold themes (all of them, by default) to the contrast rules: words at least 4.5:1 against what they sit
on, dimmed text 3:1; a focused border under 3:1 against its window is reported as advice.
.TP
.BR scheme " " options | json | preview | keep " [" \fICHOICE\fR = \fIVALUE\fR ...]
A colour scheme built from choices (paper, tone, accent, windows, shade, terminal, terminal_shade, text,
contrast), as the settings
build it;
.B keep
writes the choices to [scheme] in config.toml and the theme
.BR custom .
.TP
.BR "theme revert" " \fINAME\fR"
A shipped theme as shipped again: your copy of it removed.
.TP
.B launcher
Open or close the launcher (Super+D): the applications as a register on the sheet, found as you type
and launched through the compositor.
.TP
.BR menu " [" "\-\-prompt \fITEXT\fR" ]
A menu of the lines on standard input in the launcher's register; the line chosen on standard output.
Exits 1 when the menu was closed without a choice, 2 when the shell could not show it.
.TP
.B settings
Open or close the settings (Super+T): the colour scheme, from the themes or built from choices, and
the wallpaper, the margin cells and the title block's tint.
.TP
.BR wallpaper " [" \fIFILE\fR "|" none ]
A picture in the frame, or none.
.TP
.B wallpaper json
The wallpaper, the margins and the title block's tint as set, and the pictures to choose from
(~/Pictures/Wallpapers and the system's backgrounds), as JSON.
.TP
.BR "wallpaper set" " \fINAME\fR=\fIVALUE\fR ..."
Any of picture (a file, or none), margins (paper, ambient), tint, fit (fill, fit, centre), position
(centre, top, bottom, left, right), brightness, wash and blur (millimetres), in one write, as the
settings' wallpaper page sets them.
.TP
.BR margins " " paper "|" ambient
With a wallpaper: the margins in the theme's paper or in the picture's colours.
.TP
.BR lock-on-sleep " [" on "|" off "|" toggle ]
Lock before every suspend, until turned off or the next login.
.TP
.BR audio " " save "|" restore
Keep the default output's volume and mute from one session to the next.
.TP
.B readings
Print the readings the shell polls, as JSON.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH COMPOSITOR REQUESTS
.B draftpaper-compositor msg
talks to the running compositor over
.BR $DRAFTPAPER_SOCKET :
.BR version ", " outputs ", " workspaces ", " windows ", " focused-output ,
.B subscribe
.IR EVENT ...,
or any action a key can be bound to, such as
.B workspace 3
or
.BR "power off" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/draftpaper/config.toml
Settings: theme, margin, wallpaper, title block, custom cells, alerts, idle, applications, input,
animations, outputs, key bindings. Read again when it changes.
.TP
.I ~/.config/draftpaper/themes/
Themes of your own, beside those in
.IR /usr/share/draftpaper/themes/ .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/draftpaper/
Generated files: the shell's theme, fuzzel's, the lock screen's and swayidle's configuration, and the
launcher's count of launches (launcher.json).
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/draftpaper/README.md
The keys and every setting.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qs (1),
.BR swaylock (1),
.BR swayidle (1),
.BR fuzzel (1),
.BR kanshi (1),
.BR wlr-randr (1)
